.TH "SSHRC" "1" "" "2.1.0" "sshrc"
.SH NAME
\f[B]sshrc\f[R] \- Bring your dotfiles with you when you ssh
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]sshrc\f[R] [\f[I]OPTION\f[R]]\&...
\f[I]SSH options and arguments\f[R]\&...
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]sshrc\f[R] \f[B]\-z\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]sshrc\f[R] initiates a normal \f[B]ssh\f[R] session, but also
copies the contents of your local \f[B]$SSHRC_HOME\f[R] directory to the
remote server and sources the remote copy of \f[I]$SSHRC_HOME/sshrc\f[R]
in lieu of any remote shell files.
.IP
.EX
local$ echo \[dq]echo welcome\[dq] > $SSHRC_HOME/sshrc/sshrc
local$ sshrc server
welcome
server$

local$ echo \[dq]alias ..=\[aq]cd ..\[aq]\[dq] > $SSHRC_HOME/sshrc/sshrc
local$ sshrc server
server$ type ..
\&.. is aliased to \[ga]cd ..\[aq]
.EE
.PP
For simplicity, your \f[B]$SSHRC_HOME\f[R]\f[I]/sshrc\f[R] file will be
referred to as your \f[I]sshrc_file\f[R].
You can use this file to set environment variables, define functions,
and run post\-login commands, allowing you to use the same configuration
on multiple remote servers without needing to configure them
individually.
.PP
\f[B]sshrc\f[R] creates a unique remote copy of your local
\f[B]sshrc\f[R] configuration each time you login.
This makes \f[B]sshrc\f[R] useful if you share a remote account with
multiple users as each user can login to the same remote account and
have a copy of their local \f[B]sshrc\f[R] configuration without
conflict.
.PP
By default the initial shell spawned by \f[B]sshrc\f[R] does not source
login shell files (\f[I]/etc/profile\f[R], \f[I]~/.profile\f[R],
\f[I]~/.bash_profile\f[R], etc.).
Use the \f[B]\-j\f[R] option to load these files.
.SS Message of the Day
By default \f[B]sshrc\f[R] will attempt to print the remote message of
the day and the last login time.
This can be suppressed by creating a remote \f[I]\[ti]/.hushlogin\f[R]
file (per \f[B]login(1)\f[R]) or be creating a
\f[I]$SSHRC_HOME/.hushlogin\f[R] file.
.SS Bash Completion
To enable local \f[B]bash\f[R] completion for \f[B]sshrc\f[R], add
\f[B]complete \-F comp_cmd_ssh sshrc\f[R] to your local
\f[I]\[ti]/.bashrc\f[R].
.SH OPTIONS
Any options or arguments not used by \f[B]sshrc\f[R] itself are passed
directly to \f[B]ssh\f[R].
Because of how \f[B]ssh\f[R] works, the \f[B]\-t\f[R] option is
automatically used.
.TP
\f[B]\-j\f[R]
force \f[B]sshrc\f[R] to process local login shell files before
processing your \f[I]sshrc_file\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-z\f[R]
print size of the compressed \f[B]sshrc\f[R] loader and configuration
files and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-Z\f[R] \f[I]SIZE\f[R]
use \f[I]SIZE\f[R] as the maximum size of the compressed \f[B]sshrc\f[R]
loader and configuartion files in KiB (Default: 1024 KiB).
\f[B]sshrc\f[R] will exit without connecting if this size is exceeded.
See \f[B]CAVEATS\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-u\f[R]
setup \f[B]tmux\f[R] support using the unique system identifier
.TP
\f[B]\-U\f[R] \f[I]ID\f[R]
setup \f[B]tmux\f[R] support using \f[I]ID\f[R] as the unique identifier
.TP
\f[B]\-d\f[R]
display the unique system identifier used by \f[B]sshrc\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R]
display help
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[B]$SSHRC_HOME/sshrc\f[R]
This file will be sourced by the remote server instead of the normal
interactive shell files.
For simplicity, this file is referred to as the \f[I]sshrc_file\f[R]
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]$SSHRC_HOME\f[R]
The contents of this directory are copied to the remote server.
The default value is \f[B]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R]\f[I]/sshrc\f[R]
.SH TMUX SUPPORT
Normally, using \f[B]tmux\f[R] in an \f[B]sshrc\f[R] session uses the
remote \f[I]\[ti]/.tmux.conf\f[R] and spawns shells that read your
remote shell files.
\f[B]sshrc\f[R] can optionally create a framework that reads
\f[B]$SSHRC_HOME\f[R]\f[I]/tmux.conf\f[R] if found and spawn shells that
read your \f[B]sshrc\f[R] file.
.PP
Simply use the \f[B]\-u\f[R] option when starting \f[B]sshrc\f[R] and
run \f[B]tmux\f[R] normally.
If you do not have a \f[B]$SSHRC_HOME\f[R]\f[I]/tmux.conf\f[R] file the
local \f[I]\[ti]/.tmux.conf\f[R] will be read instead.
.PP
\f[B]NOTE:\f[R] Do not set the \f[B]tmux\f[R] \f[I]default\-command\f[R]
option as this is set by \f[B]sshrc\f[R].
.PP
Any remote \f[B]tmux\f[R] sessions created after connecting with
\f[B]sshrc \-u\f[R] will persist across multiple invocations of
\f[B]sshrc\f[R].
These \f[B]tmux\f[R] sessions will maintain their own shared copy of
your \f[B]sshrc\f[R] configuration even after the initial
\f[B]sshrc\f[R] session exists.
This shared copy is only after the final \f[B]tmux\f[R] session is
closed.
.PP
When setting up \f[B]tmux\f[R] support \f[B]sshrc\f[R] uses a locally
stored unique identifier and provides the remote \f[B]tmux_id\f[R] and
\f[B]tmuxrc\f[R] commands to facilitate sharing \f[B]tmux\f[R] sessions
betwee multiple locations or users.
See \f[B]EXAMPLES\f[R] for specifics.
.SH CAVEATS
\f[B]sshrc\f[R] works by passing a loader script and the compressed
contents of \f[B]$SSHRC_HOME\f[R] as a base64 encoded as the \[lq]remote
command\[rq] of the \f[B]ssh\f[R] session.
Because the remote server has limits on the length of the remote command
it will accept, \f[B]sshrc\f[R] refuses to even attempt to login if it
attempts to upload > 1024 KiB.
This maximum size can be changed with the \f[B]\-Z\f[R] option.
.PP
Even if this limit is not exceeded, a large \f[B]$SSHRC_HOME\f[R] will
slow down your login times.
.PP
Use the \f[B]\-z\f[R] option to determine the total size of data that
\f[B]sshrc\f[R] will upload.
.SH BUGS
\f[B]sshrc\f[R] should work with any POSIX\-compliant Bourne shell
derivative except the BusyBox shell.
Any failures with a supported shell are bugs and should be reported.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Using $SSHRC
When an \f[B]sshrc\f[R] session begins the remote environment variable
\f[B]$SSHRC\f[R] is set to the path to the remote copy of your
\f[B]$SSHRC_HOME\f[R].
This can be used as needed to configure environment variables or aliases
to force remote commands to use the \f[B]sshrc\f[R] version of their
configurations.
.PP
For example, add an alias to your \f[B]sshrc\f[R] file to have
\f[B]vim\f[R] use \f[I]$SSHRC/vimrc\f[R] instead of
\f[I]\[ti]/.vimrc\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
alias vim=\[aq]vim \-Nu $SSHRC/vimrc\[aq]
.EE
.PP
(\f[CR]\-N\f[R] forces \f[CR]\[aq]nocompatible\[aq]\f[R] because
\f[CR]\-u\f[R] sets \f[CR]\[aq]compatible\[aq]\f[R], which is normally
what you want.
Remove the \f[CR]\-N\f[R] if you \f[B]want\f[R] vi\-compatibility.)
.SS Tmux
To connect to your own \f[B]sshrc\f[R]\-associated \f[B]tmux\f[R]
session from a different machine, you first get the unique identifier
associated with your \f[B]sshrc\f[R] sessions by using the \f[B]\-d\f[R]
option locally or the \f[B]tmux_id\f[R] command remotely.
.PP
Then, form a different machine, use the \f[B]\-U\f[R] \f[I]ID\f[R]
option instead of \f[B]\-u\f[R] to allow connecting to your previous
\f[B]tmux\f[R] sessions.
.IP
.EX
# Login with tmux support
box\-1$ sshrc \-u server

# Get your unique identifier prior to detaching
server$ tmux_id
ABCDEF

# From another system, login with tmux support
# connected to the previous sessions
box\-2$ sshrc \-U ABCDEF server
server$ tmux attach
# you are attached to the previously detached session
.EE
.PP
If multiple users use \f[B]sshrc\f[R] to log into the same remote
account and want to share \f[B]tmux\f[R] sessions, one user should run
\f[B]sshrc \-u\f[R] normally and pass their unique identifier to the
other users.
Other uses will then connect and use the remote \f[B]tmuxrc\f[R] command
instead of \f[B]tmux\f[R] to connect to any shared session.
.PP
\f[B]NOTE:\f[R] Each user does not get access to their own
\f[B]sshrc\f[R] configuration files.
.IP
.EX
# Bob gets his local identifier
bob\[at]local$ sshrc \-d
ABCDEF

# Bob logs in with tmux support
bob\[at]local$ sshrc \-u server

# Bob starts a remote tmux session that he wants to share
bob\[at]server$ tmux
# inside a tmux session

# Alice logs in using sshrc but does not use \-u
alice\[at]local$ sshrc server

# Alice runs tmuxrc remotely with Bob\[aq]s identifer
alice\[at]server$ tmuxrc ABCDEF [tmux options and commands]
# inside the shared tmux session
.EE
